// WHAT THIS IS FOR: statement and argument mentions inside a CLAIM'S OWN TEXT.
//
// Argdown resolves bold, italic and links away into plain text, but leaves mentions exactly as
// written. So a claim whose text mentions another claim used to draw as
//
//     We should heed @[Voice of the People] and the @<Turnover Argument>.
//
// with the sigils and brackets intact -- which reads as a syntax error the viewer failed to
// handle rather than as the reference it is.
//
// WHY THIS FIXTURE HAD TO BE WRITTEN. The audit cited three of Argdown's own sample maps as
// evidence, counting nine mentions in the populism map and six in Greenspan's. Every one of them
// is in the FREE PROSE under a section heading, which never becomes a node, so not one of those
// files exercises the defect at all. The bug is real and those files do not show it. This is the
// smallest file that does.
//
// WHAT TO SEE: two boxes. `Main` reads "We should heed Voice of the People and the Turnover
// Argument." -- the names in plain text, no `@`, no brackets. If sigils are visible, the
// resolution in `toGraph` has regressed.

[Voice of the People]: The people speak with one voice.

[Main]: We should heed @[Voice of the People] and the @<Turnover Argument>.
    + [Voice of the People]
    + <Turnover Argument>

<Turnover Argument>: Turnover in office is what keeps the voice honest.
    + [Voice of the People]
